  "why": "The statement asks for the classes of ODEs on which Lyapunov's second method, classical or generalised, is necessary and sufficient for (asymptotic) stability, so each converse theorem for a named class answers one restriction and is a case. The classical converses (Massera, Malkin, Kurzweil, Teel–Praly, Zubov) are pre-snapshot cases. The close call is Della Rossa–Tanwani (2024, ESAIM COCV): a necessary-and-sufficient multiple-Lyapunov-function characterisation for switched nonlinear systems under average dwell-time. Switched systems are piecewise-defined ODEs, and multiple Lyapunov functions are a standard generalised form of the method, so I counted it as a case rather than a variant; journal acceptance counts as digested. Liu–Fitzsimmons 2026 refutes a conjecture about polynomial Lyapunov functions, and it exhibits a smooth Lyapunov function for the same field, so on the statement's question it is a variant. Narter–Incer concerns a graded, stronger notion of stability, so it is also a variant. The AI papers construct Lyapunov functions for particular systems, so their scope is none. On their own quotes the trained models produced those functions, which makes their role generated, but none of these events bears on the class question. Lalik's and Serry's networks only estimate or approximate, so their role is none. Lalik's January 2024 month and Lai–Motta's bare year 2024 cannot be placed either side of 26 January.",
